## What it does

```jsx
const Row = memo(function Row({ user, onSelect }) {
  return <li onClick={() => onSelect(user.id)}>{user.name}</li>;
});
```

Before re-rendering, React shallow-compares each prop with `Object.is`. If all match, it reuses the previous output.

---

## Why it usually fails

```jsx
function List({ users }) {
  return users.map((user) => (
    <Row
      key={user.id}
      user={user}
      onSelect={(id) => select(id)}    // ❌ new function every render
      style={{ padding: 8 }}            // ❌ new object every render
    />
  ));
}
```

Both props fail the comparison every single time. `memo` now runs a comparison that always returns false, then re-renders anyway — strictly slower than not using it.

```jsx
// ✅ Stabilise every prop, or memo is pointless
const STYLE = { padding: 8 };                      // hoisted constant
const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => select(id), [select]);

<Row user={user} onSelect={handleSelect} style={STYLE} />
```

> **`memo` is all-or-nothing.** One unstable prop out of ten defeats it entirely.

---

## When it is worth it

| Good candidate | Poor candidate |
|----------------|----------------|
| Large list rows | A component rendering one `<span>` |
| Charts, maps, editors | Anything cheap |
| Component whose parent re-renders often for unrelated reasons | Component that re-renders because its own props changed |
| Props are primitives or stable references | Props include inline objects |

---

## Custom comparison

```jsx
const Row = memo(RowImpl, (prev, next) => prev.user.id === next.user.id);
```

Use sparingly. It is easy to compare too little and ship a component that never updates — a genuinely nasty bug to track down. Note the return value is inverted relative to `shouldComponentUpdate`: returning `true` means "equal, skip the render".

---

## Usually better than memo

**Move state down** so the expensive component is not in the re-rendering subtree at all:

```jsx
// Before: typing re-renders <Chart>
function Page() {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');
  return <><input value={q} onChange={…} /><Chart /></>;
}

// After: the search box owns its state; Chart is outside it
function Page() {
  return <><SearchBox /><Chart /></>;
}
```

No memo, no useCallback, no comparison cost — and it cannot silently stop working when someone adds a prop.

> The **React Compiler** memoises automatically. Where it is enabled, manual `memo` largely stops being necessary.

## Why memo will not save you

```jsx
// 10,000 <li> elements. Every one is a real DOM node the browser
// must lay out, paint and keep in memory.
{items.map((item) => <Row key={item.id} item={item} />)}
```

`memo` prevents *re-rendering*. It does nothing about the initial mount of 10,000 nodes, or the layout cost the browser pays on every reflow. The fix has to reduce the node count.

---

## Virtualisation

```jsx
import { useVirtualizer } from '@tanstack/react-virtual';

function BigList({ items }) {
  const parentRef = useRef(null);

  const virtualizer = useVirtualizer({
    count: items.length,
    getScrollElement: () => parentRef.current,
    estimateSize: () => 48,
    overscan: 5,                       // render a few extra above and below
  });

  return (
    <div ref={parentRef} style={{ height: 600, overflow: 'auto' }}>
      {/* Spacer gives the scrollbar the correct total height */}
      <div style={{ height: virtualizer.getTotalSize(), position: 'relative' }}>
        {virtualizer.getVirtualItems().map((virtualRow) => (
          <div
            key={virtualRow.key}
            style={{
              position: 'absolute',
              top: 0,
              transform: `translateY(${virtualRow.start}px)`,
              height: virtualRow.size,
              width: '100%',
            }}
          >
            {items[virtualRow.index].name}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

The spacer div supplies the scroll height; absolute positioning places each visible row.

---

## What you give up

| Cost | Mitigation |
|------|------------|
| Ctrl+F does not find off-screen rows | Provide your own search |
| Variable row heights are harder | Dynamic measurement — supported, but slower |
| Print shows only visible rows | Render unvirtualised for print |
| Screen readers may need help | `aria-rowcount`, `aria-rowindex` |
| More complex code | Only virtualise when you need to |

---

## Cheaper options to try first

1. **Paginate.** Users rarely want 10,000 rows at once, and pagination is far simpler.
2. **Reduce per-row cost.** A row with six nested divs and an inline SVG costs six times a simple one.
3. **`content-visibility: auto`.** A CSS property that lets the browser skip rendering off-screen content — one line, no library, good for moderately long lists.

---

## The threshold

Below roughly 100 rows, do not bother. Between 100 and 1,000, measure — it depends on row complexity. Above 1,000, virtualise.

## The four common causes

**1. New references as props**

```jsx
// ❌ Both are new objects each render, defeating memo on Child
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} onSave={() => save()} />

// ✅
const CONFIG = { theme: 'dark' };                      // hoisted
const onSave = useCallback(() => save(), [save]);
<Child config={CONFIG} onSave={onSave} />
```

**2. Unstable context value**

```jsx
// ❌ Every consumer re-renders whenever the provider does
<Ctx.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>

// ✅
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
<Ctx.Provider value={value}>
```

**3. State too high in the tree**

```jsx
// ❌ Typing re-renders the entire page
function Page() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  return <><SearchInput value={query} onChange={setQuery} /><HugeTable /></>;
}

// ✅ State lives inside the component that uses it
function Page() {
  return <><SearchSection /><HugeTable /></>;
}
```

**4. Mutation, then set**

```jsx
items.push(newItem);
setItems(items);        // ❌ same reference — no re-render at all
setItems([...items]);   // ✅
```

---

## Structure beats memoisation

**Move state down** — the single most effective fix, shown above.

**Pass children through** — elements created by a grandparent do not re-render when the parent's state changes:

```jsx
function Panel({ children }) {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  return <div>{open && <Details />}{children}</div>;   // children unaffected
}

<Panel><ExpensiveThing /></Panel>   {/* ExpensiveThing never re-renders */}
```

**Split context by change frequency** so fast-changing values do not drag slow-changing consumers along.

These are better than `memo` because they cannot silently break. A `memo` that stops working when someone adds an inline prop gives no warning at all.

---

## Finding them

React DevTools → settings → **Highlight updates when components render**. Type in one field: if borders flash across the whole page, you have a cascade. The Profiler's "why did this render?" then tells you which prop changed.

---

## The perspective worth keeping

Most re-renders are cheap. React re-runs the function and, finding the output unchanged, touches no DOM. Chasing every re-render adds complexity for no user-visible gain.

> Optimise re-renders when the profiler says a specific interaction is slow. Not before.

## The problem it solves

Manual memoisation is tedious, easy to get wrong, and easy to break:

```jsx
// Before — plumbing, not logic
const sorted = useMemo(() => sort(items), [items]);
const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => select(id), [select]);
const Row = memo(RowImpl);
```

The common failure is not forgetting to memoise — it is memoising *incompletely*, so one unstable prop quietly defeats the whole thing.

```jsx
// After — the compiler inserts the equivalent memoisation
const sorted = sort(items);
const handleSelect = (id) => select(id);
```

---

## How it works

It is a **Babel plugin** that runs at build time. It analyses data flow through each component, works out which values depend on which, and emits memoised code. There is no runtime library and no change to how you write components — only what ships.

---

## Why purity is the precondition

The compiler can only skip recomputation if recomputing would have produced the same result. That requires:

- No mutation of props or state
- No side effects during render
- Same inputs producing the same output

If it cannot prove a component is safe, it **leaves that component alone**. It fails to optimise rather than failing to work — which is why adopting it is low risk.

```jsx
// ❌ Mutating a prop — the compiler bails out on this component
function List({ items }) {
  items.sort();               // mutates the caller's array
  return items.map(…);
}

// ✅ Compilable
function List({ items }) {
  const sorted = [...items].sort();
  return sorted.map(…);
}
```

---

## Adopting it

```bash
npx react-compiler-healthcheck    # reports how much of your codebase is compatible
```

The ESLint plugin flags rule violations before you enable it. Most breakage traces back to genuine bugs the compiler surfaced — components that were already relying on impure behaviour.

---

## What it does not do

| Still your job | Why |
|----------------|-----|
| Virtualising long lists | The cost is DOM nodes, not re-renders |
| Code splitting | Bundle size is a different problem |
| Avoiding waterfall requests | Data fetching, not rendering |
| Choosing the right data structure | It optimises what you wrote |

---

## How to talk about it

"It automates memoisation, but the underlying model still matters — you need to understand *why* a re-render happens to debug anything." That signals you know the compiler is an optimisation, not a replacement for understanding rendering.
